Usage: split [OPTION]... [INPUT [PREFIX]]

Output fixed-size pieces of INPUT to PREFIXaa, PREFIXab, ...

By default, split puts 1000 lines of INPUT (or stdin) into each output file.

Mandatory arguments to long options are mandatory for short options too.

SIZE may have a multiplier suffix: b for 512, K for 1K, M for 1M, G for 1G, etc.

Note: This implementation supports common GNU line, byte, line-byte, number, filter, separator, and suffix options.

OPTIONS:
  -b, --bytes ARG               put SIZE bytes per output file
  -C, --line-bytes ARG          put at most SIZE bytes of complete lines per
                                output file
  -l, --lines ARG               put NUMBER lines per output file
  -n, --number ARG              generate CHUNKS output files; see explanation
                                below
  -d, --numeric-suffixes[=ARG]  use numeric suffixes instead of alphabetic
  -x, --hex-suffixes[=ARG]      use hexadecimal suffixes
  -a, --suffix-length ARG       use suffixes of length N (default 2)
      --additional-suffix ARG   append SUFFIX to output file names
      --filter ARG              write to shell COMMAND; file name is $FILE
  -e, --elide-empty-files       do not generate empty output files
      --verbose                 print a diagnostic just before each output file
                                is opened
  -t, --separator ARG           use SEP instead of newline as the record
                                separator; '\0' (zero) specifies the NUL
                                character
  -u, --unbuffered              immediately copy input to output with '-n r/...'
      --help                    display this help and exit
  -V, --version                 output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
